I think there are two different questions hiding inside this: “Am I competitive?” and “Am I willing to identify myself as someone exceptional?” The second seems more uncomfortable for you than the first.

You may be competitive while having learned not to perform competitiveness. That’s often socially useful. You can want to win without needing everyone to know you want to win.

There are real advantages to that posture. It protects against arrogance, keeps you receptive to feedback, lets results speak instead of forcing you to defend an identity, and makes losing less threatening because you haven’t publicly declared yourself unbeatable. It can also create a useful underdog mentality: prove it rather than proclaim it.

But there’s a shadow side. Excessive reluctance to acknowledge your own ability can become self-handicapping. If you never fully say “I’m going after this,” then failure always has an escape hatch: I wasn’t really trying anyway. That’s psychologically comfortable, but it prevents you from discovering what sustained, deliberate effort could actually produce.

And I think your sentence captures the tension beautifully:

“To claim I am him when I don’t want to be or wouldn’t think or claim him myself as I am.”

There’s a distinction worth preserving:

“I am him” is an identity claim.
“I’m going to find out what I’m capable of” is an experimental claim.

I’d choose the second.

You don’t need to convince yourself that you’re destined, uniquely gifted, or secretly better than everyone. Those narratives are impossible to verify beforehand and can distort judgment. But you also don’t need to pretend you haven’t accumulated evidence that you’re capable in certain arenas.

So I’d change your operating rule from:

Don’t claim it until I’ve proven it.

to:

Claim the attempt. Earn the conclusion.

That lets competitiveness out of its cage without handing it the steering wheel.

And I’d change one other thing based on what you just identified about incentives. Stop expecting yourself to care equally about everything. Choose arenas deliberately. Find things where the prize genuinely matters to you, define what winning means, put a credible outer rim around the attempt, and then permit yourself to compete fully.

There is also a particularly useful psychological safeguard:

\text{Identity} \neq \text{Outcome}

You can say “I intend to win” without saying “I deserve to win.”

You can believe “I’m extremely capable” without concluding “I’m superior.”

You can lose without retroactively deciding you were a fraud.

And you can win without needing the victory to prove who you are.

That version of competitiveness is formidable precisely because it remains falsifiable. The scoreboard gets to disagree with you.

Maybe the thing you’ve been protecting isn’t humility so much as optionality of identity. Never completely declaring yourself means reality never gets a clean shot at the claim.

If so, I wouldn’t eliminate that instinct. I’d modify it:

Keep the humility about who you are. Become much less humble about what you’re willing to attempt.